<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Lloyd Garrison</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Lloyd Garrison was an American abolitionist, journalist and social reformer who founded the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberator</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1831 and published it until slavery was abolished in 1865. Born in Newburyport, Massachusetts, he rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a> and gradual abolition, demanding the immediate and uncompensated emancipation of the enslaved. He organised the New-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> Anti-Slavery Society in 1832 and helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society in 1833. He also supported women's rights and in the 1870s became a prominent voice for women's suffrage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison.jpg" alt="William Lloyd Garrison" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Lloyd_Garrison,_abolitionist,_journalist,_and_editor_of_The_Liberator_LCCN2017660623_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Garrison was the youngest son of immigrants from the British colony of New Brunswick. His father, Abijah Garrison, a merchant-sailing pilot and master, moved the family to Newburyport in 1806 but lost work after the Embargo Act of 1807 and deserted the family in 1808. His mother, Frances Maria Lloyd, began calling the boy by his middle name to keep her family name; she died in 1823. He worked as a typesetter, which helped him later run his newspaper, and he often composed his editorials directly in type.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At twenty-five he joined the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> movement, later crediting John Rankin's Letters on Slavery of 1826. For a short time he was associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, which promoted resettling free Black Americans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. By late 1829 or 1830 he rejected colonisation and publicly apologised for his error. In 1829 he became co-editor with Benjamin Lundy of the Quaker newspaper Genius of Universal Emancipation in Baltimore. There he moved from Lundy's gradualism to a demand for immediate and complete emancipation, and he ran a column, The Black List, reporting the cruelties of slavery.</p><p>In 1831 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> and co-founded the weekly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberator</a> with Isaac Knapp. In January 1832 he organised the New-England Anti-Slavery Society, and in December 1833 abolitionists from ten states founded the American Anti-Slavery Society. His appeals for women to take part sparked controversy over women's right to a political voice. In December 1837 he announced that The Liberator would support the rights of woman to their utmost extent. In 1834 he married Helen Eliza Benson; they had two daughters and five sons, of whom two died as children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He promoted what he called no-governmentism, rejecting the validity of a government engaged in war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. He began as an advocate of Christian pacifism, but at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> he accepted armed struggle as a means of abolition and supported President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>. In May 1865 he proposed dissolving the American Anti-Slavery Society, but Wendell Phillips argued that its task was not complete until Black Southerners had full political and civil equality, and the resolution was defeated 118 to 48. Garrison resigned and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberator</a> at the end of 1865. He died on 24 May 1879. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> was influenced by his writings and published a short biography of him in 1904.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Lloyd Garrison” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the United States <b>William Lloyd Garrison</b> declared in the first issue of The Liberator in 1831: “I will not equivocate — I will not excuse — I will not retreat a single inch — and I will be heard.”</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newburyport, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.81,-70.88&amp;year=1853" aria-label="Newburyport on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2837 643 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Lloyd Garrison"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3287 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3287 913h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.81,-70.88&amp;year=1853">What was here in 1853? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am in earnest—I will not equivocate—I will not excuse—I will not retreat a single inch—AND I WILL BE HEARD.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Public, first issue of The Liberator, 1 January 1831 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14555/pg14555-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I will be as harsh as truth, and as uncompromising as justice. On this subject, I do not wish to think, or speak, or write, with moderation.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Public, first issue of The Liberator, 1 January 1831 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14555/pg14555-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So perish all compromises with tyranny! And let all the people say, Amen!&#8221;</p><footer>On burning a copy of the Constitution, Framingham, 4 July 1854 &#183; original &#183; as quoted in Archibald Grimké's biography (1891) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14555/pg14555-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My country is the world! my countrymen are all mankind…&#8221;</p><footer>Motto of The Liberator &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14555/pg14555-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
